  <h2 id="who-is-it-for">Who Is the Shot For?</h2>
  <p>Anyone receiving treatment for AUD or opioid addiction can be a candidate for Vivitrol. A doctor should still conduct an assessment and review a person&rsquo;s treatment history and plan before approving it.</p>
  <p>Some people shouldn&rsquo;t use this medication, including anyone:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Using opioid-containing medicines or illicit opioids</li>
    <li>Experiencing alcohol or opioid withdrawal symptoms</li>
    <li>Allergic to naltrexone or any of the ingredients in Vivitrol or the liquid used to mix it</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Access to Vivitrol isn&rsquo;t always equal, either. Among Medicaid patients treated for opioid use disorder, one study found non-Hispanic Black, non-Hispanic American Indian/Alaska Native/Asian/Hawaiian/Pacific Islander, and Hispanic patients were all significantly less likely to receive Vivitrol than non-Hispanic White patients [3].</p>

  <h2 id="how-it-works">How Does the Vivitrol Shot Work?</h2>
  <p>Vivitrol can only be given once a person has fully detoxed from alcohol and hasn&rsquo;t had a drink for 7&ndash;10 days.</p>
  <p>Because Vivitrol is an opioid antagonist, it binds to opioid and reward receptors in the brain. If a person drinks alcohol while on Vivitrol, it can&rsquo;t attach to those receptors and produce pleasurable feelings. Without the &ldquo;high,&rdquo; people are less likely to repeat alcohol-seeking and using behaviors. Over time, the blocked receptors help diminish cravings and lower the risk of relapse.</p>

