  <h2 id="side-effects">Are There Side Effects of the Vivitrol Shot?</h2>
  <p>Common side effects of Vivitrol include nausea, sleepiness, headache, dizziness, vomiting, decreased appetite, painful joints, muscle cramps, cold symptoms, trouble sleeping, and toothache. In most cases, these symptoms improve within a few days and don&rsquo;t repeat with future doses.</p>

  <figure class="bp-infographic">
    <img src="/images/blog/infographics/vivitrol-side-effects.svg" alt="Common Vivitrol side effects include nausea, sleepiness, headache, dizziness, joint pain, and cold symptoms; serious side effects needing prompt care include allergic reaction, shortness of breath, and depressed mood or suicidal thoughts" loading="lazy" width="760" height="420" />
    <figcaption>Most side effects are mild, but some warrant an immediate call to your care team.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>More serious side effects include an allergic reaction that may trigger pneumonia&mdash;warning signs are shortness of breath, wheezing, or a persistent cough. Signs of other allergic reactions include a skin rash, chest pain, feeling faint, and swelling of the eyes, mouth, or tongue. Contact a medical professional immediately if you experience any of these.</p>
  <p>Depressed mood, suicidal thoughts, and suicidal behavior are also possible. One study found people taking Vivitrol were about twice as likely to experience depressed mood as the placebo group [2]. Contact your care provider if you&rsquo;re sleeping much more or less than usual, feel hopeless, are angry or irritable, notice appetite changes, or can&rsquo;t concentrate.</p>

  <h2 id="comprehensive-care">Vivitrol as Part of Comprehensive Care for AUD</h2>
  <p>Vivitrol can be an important stage of treatment for alcohol addiction, but it works best as part of a wider, comprehensive care plan. Evidence-based therapies such as CBT and dialectical behavior therapy (DBT) address the past experiences and thought patterns that fuel addiction, and teach healthier coping skills to sustain recovery.</p>
  <p>Wellness activities support physical, mental, and spiritual healing alongside medication. Yoga, mindfulness, meditation, and creative therapies like art and music therapy help people express themselves in new ways, connect more meaningfully with others, and build self-esteem.</p>
  <p>Combined, these approaches&mdash;plus Vivitrol, when appropriate&mdash;support more complete healing. If naltrexone in its daily oral form is a better fit for someone&rsquo;s situation, <a href="/blog/does-naltrexone-help-with-alcohol-withdrawal/">that version has its own considerations</a> worth understanding too.</p>

